\version "2.24.0"
% c182 (A&S Unit 5, PDF 76, 81, 83): 252 last eighth of the P line E6 (was G6: G-F octaves with the S head into 253); 249 Cb F1 (was F2, a seventh down to G1); 249.3 Ob 2 and Tpt 2 A4 (was B4 against Tpt 3's C5); 261 Ob 2 keeps C5 (was A flat 4: the doubled seventh C of the half-diminished 6/5 fell C-A flat in octaves with Hn 4); C is the Ger6's common tone, F4 at 262.
% c129 (Rimsky-Korsakov PDF 112: in difficult cases mark the wood-wind one degree louder than the brass; Ex. 166, PDF 335: the hymn's ff statement has full wood-wind ff over ff brass): 257 the wood-winds re-enter f (were mf) and reach ff at 259 (were f), still after their rest at 255-256 and still growing to fff at 263, so the crescendo by addition (c033, c038) is kept one level up and the flutes' top octave of the chorale line is heard over the ff trumpets.
% c128 (expression; Adler ch. 11, PDF 38-40: Tchaikovsky 4/i breaks the climax off to the low strings before the next tutti; ch. 15, PDF 190: a loud chord cut off over a line already sounding; PDF 192: seams must not show): 264 the harmony winds and brass and the timpani release on beat 1, the woodwinds and Tpt 1-2 (chorale top line, length kept) diminuendo through 264, Vc/Cb diminuendo on the held C, so the strings' scale figure (bars 3-4 in C major) is heard as at bars 3-4; 257-258 Vln II, Vla, Vc, Cb crescendo to 259's ff with the winds; 247 Cbsn ff with the tuba it doubles; 277 B.cl, Cbsn, B.tbn hairpins end at their rest.
% c126 (playability; Adler ch. 6-7, PDF 188: Brahms 1/iv dovetails the flutes so one breathes while the other holds, 'without diminishing the intensity'; PDF 175 and 198: oboists must be given frequent rests; PDF 230: low contrabassoon notes take much breath, give periodic rests; PDF 198: C6-F6 on the oboe is thin and hard to control): 257-264 the woodwind (and brass) chorale held 8 (10) bars mf<fff with no breath; staggered breath marks at tie ends: Fl 1 after 258 and 261, Cl 1-2, Cbsn, Tpt 1-2, Tbn 1-2, tuba after 258; Ob 1-2, B.cl., Bn 1-2, Hn 1-2, B.tbn after 260 (Vln I, Tpt 3, Hn 3-4 and the other group cover each gap). Fl 2's arpeggio 257-262 drops its last eighth at 259 and 261 (Vln II keeps the figure) so it can breathe before the fff E5 at 263. Cbsn and tuba breathe after 250. Ob 1 rests 265-268 (G5-E6 leap ff in the thin register, head kept by Fl 1-2, Cl 1, Vln I-II; the oboe had played 247-264 with two bars' rest) and re-enters a2 with Ob 2 at 269.
% c123 (form and pacing; Rimsky-Korsakov ch. IV, PDF 118: a recurring idea is varied by alteration of details; PDF 125-126: the last note of one part leads into the first of the next; PDF 116, 133: the harmonic basis is set below the melody in dynamic power; PDF 130: a long crescendo is made by adding instruments; PDF 136: after a long rest the tuba re-enters pp or ff): 268 Fl 1-2 and Vln I continue E-D-C-B into 269's C6 (the top line no longer repeats 265-266 with a three-beat hole); 265 the accompaniment (Tpt 3, tuba, timpani, Cb) f under the ff themes, Tpt 3, tuba and timpani crescendo 269-270 to ff at 271, Cb stays f 265-274 (no ff-to-f drop at the Largamente); 247 tuba ff (re-entry after 235-246).
% c122 (harmony; A&S Unit 17, PDF 268: outer-voice frames without imperfect consonances 'might sound empty unless decorated'; PDF 267: voice-leading chords carry repeated patterns; Unit 21, PDF 368: over a tonic pedal I-IV-V7-I is the usual succession and the upper parts' dissonances resolve normally): 272 the F1 peak A-G-F had only octaves over the C pedal (beat 4 a bare F over C; Tpt 3 G under the A). Now IV6/4 under it from beat 2: Hn 3 A4, Hn 4 F4, Bn 1 F4, violas div. F4-A4, and Tpt 3 A4 for the bar; into 273 every inner F steps to E (Hn 4, Bn 1, Vla) and every A to G (Tpt 3, Vla), Hn 3 A4 to C5. The violas' "div." moves from 273 to 272.
% c117 (texture; Adler ch. 15, PDF 179 and 181: a middleground figuration under a foreground theme is given mixed colour and mass, e.g. Scheherazade's 'dance figure' in piccolo, flutes and both violin sections, Holst's figuration in winds and violins): 257-262 Fl 2 doubles Vln II's arpeggio figure an octave higher (was held harmony C6-A flat5-F5, all still in Vln II, Ob 2 and the brass), so the figure is heard under the ff brass chorale.
% c117 (Adler ch. 15, PDF 184: each fragment is given to a part that is clearly heard, and a held part does not sit on the moving voice's pitches): 275 Vln II plays E5 only (unis., was div. C5-E5 tremolo), so the scherzo quotation's lower octave (Tpt 1-2, Cl 1: C5-G4-G4-C5-E5) is not absorbed by a C5 tremolo at its own pitch; C5 stays held in Hn 3.
% c087 (lens 7; Rimsky-Korsakov PDF 75 and 79): Cl 2 joins Ob 1/Cl 1 on the S head upper octave 251-254 (two horns below need three winds above in forte); Cl 2 and Bn 1 double F1 at 265-268 (Tpt + Cl + Vla over Hn + Bn + Vc).
% ALLEGRO MOLTO, bars 247-279 (c070: v01 numbers in this paragraph and the next; v01 243-262 = now 247-279 after c013/c023; the Maestoso of v01 251-255 was removed in c013, the last chorale is 255-264 at Più allegro 2 = 88 since v76; iiø6/5 with A♭ at 259-260 (c080: was described as ♭VI), Ger6 at 261, V7-I 262-263) (cyclic-notes 'Owed' items 6, 7, 3). 243-250: movement I's first-theme head in C major on top (flute 1 and
% violins I, oboe 1/clarinet 1/violins II, violas, in octaves) combined with movement I's second-theme head in the brass (trumpets, horns),
% fast, ff, over the hammered double-bass eighths: movement I's tutti layout turned to C major. 251-255: Maestoso, the chorale's last
% statement in full brass, ending C-B-C, over I6 - vi - iiø6/5 (borrowed) - bVI - V7 - I: the symphony's first root-position V7-I in C since
% movement I bar 135; trumpet 1 takes B to C, trumpet 2 has the E natural (the Don Giovanni arrival of cyclic-notes). 256-262: tonic close,
% movement I's timpani pulse, last chord ff. c003: every part 20 bars; 260 echoes the cadence (V on the eighth, C-B-C in the violins), final chord 261, fermata 262.

% c023 (form lens; A&S Unit 32): the C-major close after the last cadence (262-263) was 9 bars (263-271, about 11 s). Eight bars added, 269-276
% (old 269-271 are now 277-279): 269-272 the two heads exchanged (this movement's F1 head on top in Fl/Ob/Cl/Tpt 1-2/violins, movement I's head
% below in Hn 1-2, Bn 1, violas, cellos), mirroring 265-268; 273-276 IV6/4 - iv6/4 - I over the C pedal with C held on top (common tone in the
% soprano, A&S PDF 587) and the inner line A - A flat - G: the bar-1 motion A flat -> G (German sixth -> V) restated at the tonic level
% (A&S PDF 600-601, motive enlarged into the plan; PDF 592-593, flat VI in major). No new V-I before 277. fff only at 278 (was 270).
% c032 (harmony; open item from the scherzo critic, decided in cycle 30): 273-275 complete the chain of the closes of movements II and III
% (E flat-D-D flat-C in II, A flat; B-A sharp-A-G sharp over E - A sharp dim7 - A minor - E in III): G-F sharp-F-E over
% C (273, first half) - F sharp dim7 over the C pedal (273, second half) - F minor 6/4 (274) - C (275), in Hn 1-2 a2, Tpt 3, Ob 2, Vln II and
% Tbn 2; inner lines C-A-A flat-G (keeps the A-A flat-G of c023) and E-E flat-F-E (violas, Hn 4, Bn 1). The quotation at 275-276 then
% continues the line E-D-D flat-C, so 273-276 run 5-#4-4-3-2-b2-1. Voicing as the scherzo's coda 365-368, a major third lower.
% c034 (orchestration; Adler ch. 15, PDF 151: weight the octave the listener must hear as the line): 273-276 Vln I lower note and Ob 1 join the chain G-F sharp-F-E
% (were A-A flat-G, the line already in Fl 2, Cl 2, Tpt 2, Tbn 1, violas), so the chain is the strongest moving line under the held C6. 257 Fl/Ob/Cl re-enter mf
% (were p under ff brass and strings: Adler PDF 156, a rest before a colour enters is wasted if the entry cannot be heard).
% c032: 247 Vc and Bn 1 G4 on beat 3 (were E4: E-D under Tpt 2's B-A, parallel 5ths).
% c036 (playability; Adler ch. 6-7, PDF 175, 198, 230: wind and brass players need periodic rests): Hn 3-4 rest 265-268 (Tbn 1-2 keep the doubling under F1),
% Hn 1-2 rest 269-272 (Hn 3-4, Tbn 1, Bn 1, violas keep movement I's head), so no horn plays 243-279 without a break; Bn 2 rests 247-254 (it doubled the bass trombone at the unison);
% 257 B.cl, Bn 1-2, Cbn enter mf with the upper winds (Adler PDF 185, 210: a weak entry under ff brass is covered); 278 B.cl C3 (low C extension not required, PDF 216).
% c038 (expression; Casanelles ch. 4, PDF 148, 152): 259 the wood-wind crescendo from 257 is staged at f where strings and brass begin theirs; 273 Tpt 1-2 restate ff after their rests at 271-272; 273-279 Largamente (global.ily).
% c048 (expression; A&S Unit 3, PDF 50: the start of a note is stronger than its continuation; PDF 53: bar 2 of a four-bar group is weak): 273-274 the held C6/C5 on top (Fl 1, Tpt 1, Hn 3) is tied and the timpani roll is one tied roll 273-276 (c045 rule);
% the pedal re-strikes at 274 without accent, so the moving chain (F sharp -> F, A -> A flat) carries the accent of 274. 255-262 Hn 3 and Tpt 3 accent only the first half note of each bar (were accents on every beat, which removes the strong-weak pattern of the metre, PDF 50).
% c049 (holistic, Dvorak 7/iv; Rimsky-Korsakov PDF 157, 166, Ex. 309-310 PDF 499-500: crossing only for a short, deliberate emphasis; a doubling is redistributed when the
% register changes): 247-254 Vln II up an octave with Vln I and Fl 1-2, Ob 1-2 on the trumpets' S head (Ob 1 = Tpt 1, Ob 2 = Tpt 2): at G4-A5 the P head's lower octave
% crossed the S head in Tpt 1-2 and made seconds with it (247 D flat5/C5, 249 F5/E5, 250 D5/E5, 251 B4/C5), the defect c047 removed at 265-268.
% c049 (Dvorak 7/iv 410-415, corpus PDF 202: the tune in long notes over arpeggiated eighths in Vln II/Vla/Vc; Rimsky-Korsakov Ex. 291, PDF 469-470, the tutti form of a
% sung melody with running middle strings): 255-262 Vln II break the chorale's chords into eighths (were whole notes doubling the held brass), below the chorale's top line.
% c056 (Adler part 1 PDF 30): 273 Vln I, Vln II and violas marked div. (upper and lower lines of the chain; as double stops the held C6 over G5-F sharp5-F5 and C5 under G5-F sharp5 need one finger in two places).
% c078 (expression; Adler ch. 7, PDF 187: a tie or slur is one breath; PDF 188: give the flutist time to rest after sustained passages; PDF 198: oboe breath control makes rests mandatory): 264 winds and brass release the fff cadence chord on beat 4 (c2. r4, was a whole note tied into the 265 attacks), strings and timpani hold; 272 Fl, Ob, Cl breathe with the trumpets at the end of the allargando phrase.
% c079 (lens 9, Beethoven 3/iv: the Presto, bar 431, brings back the movement's opening run while the winds hold ff; Adler part 2 PDF 224: Ravel returns the opening colours at the end;
% PDF 213: Bizet's scale before the repeat anticipates the new scoring): 263-264 Vln I, Vln II, Vla play the bar 3-4 scale figure in C major after the cadence chord, launching 265.
% c083 (A&S Unit 15, PDF 237 Ex. 15-10b: I-(III) with 8-7-6 in an inner voice; PDF 239: III instead of a repeated I6 "when the stepwise descent 8-7-6 ... is wanted, or when
% excessive repetitions of 1 in any of the upper voices would otherwise result"; PDF 236 Ex. 15-9: III-VI-II-V-I): 256, the second bar of the chorale's G over the E bass, is iii (E-G-B)
% instead of a second bar of I6: Hn 4 C4-B3-A3 (255-257, 8-7-6), Vln II arpeggio with B, Tpt 3 E4 (was C4), violas E4 one bar early (were C5, eight repeated tonics).
% The protected chords (I6 255, vi, iiø6/5, Ger6, V7, I) and the chorale line are unchanged; 255-262 becomes I6-iii-vi-iiø6/5-Ger6-V7, a descending-fifth chain into the cadence.
% c085 (engraving; Adler ch. 2, PDF 38-39: a slur is one bow, and the louder the passage the fewer notes per bow): 263-264 the scale figure at fff is bowed 4+2+4 (c''8( b'8) | c''8( ...)) instead of 4+6 across the barline (Vln I, Vln II, Vla); 277 cellos 'div.' on C3-G3 (PDF 30: without div. the chord is a double-stop), as violins and violas are marked at 278.
% c088 (expression; Adler ch. 10, PDF 303 Ex. X-5 and PDF 318 Ex. X-46: brilliance and loudness rise with register; PDF 305 Ex. X-13, Mahler 1: f, ff, then cresc. to fff, the fff kept for the peak): 263 Fl 1 takes the chorale's last C two octaves above Tpt 1 (C7, as at 223) and Cl 2 plays 257-264 an octave higher (E5-F5-E flat5-G5-G5, out of the throat register), so the 263 chord is no longer an octave lower on top than 223 and 278; 255 Vln II, Vla, Vc, Cb f (Vln I stays ff with the chorale) and ff at 259 with the existing crescendo, so the accompaniment grows f-ff-fff like the winds (mf 257, f 259); 265 Cl 1's ff on the eighth rest, merged with Cl 2's ff (the two marks an eighth apart printed as 'ffff').
% c121 (melody; Adler ch. 3, PDF 74 Ex. III-4, Brahms 1/iv: the tune's opening bars return only after a continuation, and the restatement changes its third bar;
% this score's own exposition 11-16 answers the head with the climb and then with the head a third higher): 267-268 F1's head moves up a third (E-B-C | D-C-B-A-G
% in Tpt 1-2, Cl 2 at C5; Hn 1-2, Bn 1, Vla, Vc at C4), so the head is not heard three times at one pitch in 265-270; Tpt 3 G at 267 and F at 268 (I, then ii7 over the C pedal).
% 272 the major F1 takes its consequent's peak bar (A-G-F, as A flat-G-F at 22 and 162) instead of the antecedent's F-E-D, so its last statement reaches A6/A5;
% the seventh F resolves to E in Cl 1 at 273 (the others fall to C as before).
% c184 (orchestration; Rimsky-Korsakov ch. I, PDF 51: trumpet = trombone = 2 horns in forte, wood-wind half a horn, one string department two winds): 247 Tbn 1-2 f (were ff) on the P head's lower octave, the level of Tpt 1-2 on the S head (c084), so the two trombones three octaves under the flutes and violins no longer outweigh both heads' upper voices.
% c184 (Rimsky-Korsakov ch. III, PDF 83-84: the bass of an inverted seventh chord is not doubled in the upper parts; PDF 85: the harmonic notes must not be lacking in the upper parts): 259-260 Hn 3 A flat 3 (was F4, the iio6/5's bass F, already in Tpt 3 and violas), so the borrowed A flat has two brass (Tbn 2, Hn 3) instead of one; Hn 3 rises A flat 3 -> E flat 4 at 261 (Tbn 2 falls A flat 3 -> E flat 3, contrary). Pitch classes, top line and 261-263 unchanged.
% c186 (playability; Adler ch. 2, PDF 38 [Vc 248 mark set below: above it triggers a LilyPond spacing error]: louder and heavier toward the frog, a composer marks bow direction only to counteract the players' habit; Ex. II-19: two up-bows in a row so the accent falls on a down-bow; PDF 42: down-bows retaken with the bow lifted): 247-254 Vln I, Vln II, Vc: down-bow on 247's pickup (retaken in the eighth rest), hooked up-bows at 248 b8 and 252 e8, so the head's accents (247 E6, 248 A5, 249-251, 252-253 A6, Vln 254 G6) fall on down-bows; by plain alternation 247, 252, 253, 254 were up-bows. Pitches, slurs and dynamics unchanged.
% c186 (Adler ch. 2, PDF 25: a wind or brass player must stop to breathe; c126's staggered breaths left Hn 3-4 none, and Hn 4 plays 235-263 without a rest): Hn 3-4 breathe after 250 (with Hn 1-2, Cbsn, tuba) and after 256 (no other part breathes there; Hn 1-2, Tpt, Tbn hold).
finaleVlnOne = { r8 g''8\downbow(\ff e'''4.->) \once \override Beam.positions = #'(0 . -0.25) d'''8( des'''8 c'''8 | c'''4--) b''8\upbow( c'''8) a''4.-> g''8 | r8 g''8( f'''4.->) \once \override Beam.positions = #'(0.25 . -0.25) e'''8( d'''8 c'''8) | c'''8-. d'''-. e'''-. d'''-. g'''4.-> f'''8 | r8 g''8( e'''4.->) \once \override Beam.positions = #'(0.25 . -0.25) d'''8( c'''8 b''8 | c'''4) \once \override Beam.positions = #'(0 . -0.25) e'''8\upbow( d'''8) a'''4.-> e'''8 | f'''8( g'''8 a'''4.->) g'''8( f'''8 e'''8) | g'''2.-> r4 | g''1\ff~\cbaw | g''1 | e'''1~ | e'''1 | d'''1\<~ | d'''1 | c'''1 | b''1 | c'''4\fff g''8( f''8 e''8 d''8) c''8( b'8) | c''8( d''8 e''8 f''8) g''4-> g'4-> | r8 g''8(\ff e'''4.->) \once \override Beam.positions = #'(0.25 . -0.25) d'''8( c'''8 b''8 | c'''4->) r4 r2 | r8 g''8( e'''4.->) \once \override Beam.positions = #'(0.25 . -0.25) d'''8( c'''8 b''8 | c'''4->) e'''8( d'''8 c'''4 b''4) | c'''2->( g''4. a''8 | bes''4-.) a''8( g''8 f''4-.) e''4-. | d''4( g''8 a''8 b''4 d'''4) | a'''2-> g'''4( f'''4) | <g'' c'''>2:32->\ff^\markup \italic "div." <fis'' c'''>2:32 | <f'' c'''>1:32-> | <e'' c'''>4->\< g''8-.^\markup \italic "unis." g''8-. c'''4-. e'''4->~( | e'''4 d'''8 des'''8 c'''4-.) r4 | <e'' c'''>4->^\markup \italic "div." r8 <d'' b''>8 <e'' c'''>4-> r4 | \once \textLengthOn <c''' e''' g'''>1->\fff~^\markup \italic "div. a 3" | <c''' e''' g'''>4 r4 r2\fermata | }
% c101 (melody lens; Rimsky-Korsakov ch. II, PDF 60, distributions 2 and 3, Ex. 27 Sadko: a quick forte figure goes to Vns I+II over the violas for brilliance in the upper part; Vn I over Vn II+violas is for a fuller cantabile lower part): 263-264 Vln II joins Vln I on the scale figure (G5-B4, then up to G5), violas alone take the lower octave, so the opening run is heard over the fff wind and brass hold.
% c118 (Adler ch. 2, PDF 37: notes under one slur are one bow): 276 Vln II E-D-D flat-C on one bow, as the oboes it doubles and Vln I (was e''4 d''8( des''8) c''4-.).
finaleVlnTwo = { r8 g''8\downbow(\ff e'''4.->) \once \override Beam.positions = #'(0 . -0.25) d'''8( des'''8 c'''8 | c'''4--) b''8\upbow( c'''8) a''4.-> g''8 | r8 g''8( f'''4.->) \once \override Beam.positions = #'(0.25 . -0.25) e'''8( d'''8 c'''8) | c'''8-. d'''-. e'''-. d'''-. g'''4.-> f'''8 | r8 g''8( e'''4.->) \once \override Beam.positions = #'(0.25 . -0.25) d'''8( c'''8 b''8 | c'''4) \once \override Beam.positions = #'(0 . -0.25) e'''8\upbow( d'''8) a'''4.-> e'''8 | f'''8( g'''8 a'''4.->) g'''8( f'''8 e'''8) | g'''2.-> r4 | e'8(\f g' c'' e'') g''( e'' c'' g') | e'8( g' b' e'') g''( e'' b' g') | a'8(\< c'' e'' a'') e''( c'' a' e') | a'8( c'' e'' a'') e''( c'' a' c'') | aes'8(\ff\< d'' f'' aes'') f''( d'' aes' f') | aes'8( d'' f'' aes'') f''( d'' aes' d'') | aes'8( c'' es'' fis'') aes''( fis'' es'' c'') | g'8( b' d'' f'') g''( f'' d'' b') | <e'' g''>4\fff g''8( f''8 e''8 d''8) c''8( b'8) | c''8( d''8 e''8 f''8) g''4-> g'4-> | r8 g''8(\ff e'''4.->) d'''8( c'''8 b''8 | c'''4->) r4 r2 | r8 g''8( e'''4.->) d'''8( c'''8 b''8 | c'''4->) r4 r2 | c''2->( g'4. a'8 | bes'4-.) a'8( g'8 f'4-.) e'4-. | d'4( g'8 a'8 b'4 d''4) | a''2-> g''4( f''4) | <c'' g''>2:32->\ff^\markup \italic "div." <c'' fis''>2:32 | <c'' f''>1:32-> | e''1:32\<^\markup \italic "unis." | e''4( d''8 des''8 c''4-.) r4 | <e' c''>4->^\markup \italic "div." r8 <d' b'>8 <e' c''>4-> r4 | \once \textLengthOn <c'' e'' g''>1->\fff~^\markup \italic "div. a 3" | <c'' e'' g''>4 r4 r2\fermata | }
% c040 (cyclic lens; Adler ch. 1, PDF 19: timbre clarifies form and content; ch. 15, PDF 188-189: at-pitch doubling with an instrument of affinity): 273-276 the violas no longer
% move E4 -> E flat 4 (movement II bar 2's undoing of the thread, at the same pitch, after the thread closed in the violas at 219-220). Upper violas take the
% chain G4-F sharp4-F4-E4 with Hn 1-2 and Tpt 3, so the chain's 3 lands on E4, the violas' E of movement I bar 182 and finale 220; lower violas take C-A-A flat-G (A3-A flat3-G3) with Tbn 1.
% Every chord keeps its pitch classes (E flat at 273 stays in Hn 4 and Bn 1).
% c109 (Rimsky-Korsakov PDF 79: string and brass in unison never blend, each is heard separately; the pairs that match are Violin + Trumpet, Viola + Horn, 'Cello + Trombone; PDF 79-80: Violas/'Cellos + Cl + Horn is a natural three-group unison): 265-268 the violas play F1 at C4 with Hn 1-2, Bn 1 and the cellos instead of at C5 under two ff trumpets; the C5 octave is Tpt 1-2 + Cl 2. The scale figure at 264 now ends on G3 and steps to C4.
finaleVla = { e'2.~(\ff e'8 d'8) | c'4( a'2-- g'4) | f'4.( e'8 d'4 f'8 a'8) | c''2( b'2) | e'2.~( e'8 d'8) | c'4( a'2-- g'4) | f'4.( e'8 d'4 c'4) | b2( c'2) | c''4\f c'' c'' c'' | e'4 e' e' e' | c'4\< c' c' c' | c'4 c' c' c' | f'4\ff\< f' f' f' | f'4 f' f' f' | fis'4 fis' fis' fis' | g'4 g' g' g' | e'4\fff g'8( f'8 e'8 d'8) c'8( b8) | c'8( d'8 e'8 f'8) g'4-> g4-> | c'2->(\ff g4. a8 | bes4-.) a8( g8 f4-.) e4-. | e'2->( b4. c'8 | d'4-.) c'8( b8 a4-.) g4-. | r8 g8( e'4.->) d'8( c'8 b8 | c'4->) r4 r2 | r8 g8( e'4.->) d'8( c'8 b8 | c'4->) <f' a'>2.^\markup \italic "div." | <e' g'>2:32->\ff <a fis'>2:32 | <aes f'>1:32-> | <g e'>1:32\< | <g e'>1:32 | <g e'>4-> r8 <g d'>8 <g e'>4-> r4 | \once \textLengthOn <g e' c''>1->\fff~^\markup \italic "div. a 3" | <g e' c''>4 r4 r2\fermata | }
% c026: Tpt 1-2 rest at 271-272 (playability, brass endurance: about 90 s of ff/fff from 243 with three C6s; the fourth F1 statement stays in Fl/Ob/Cl/violins).
% c017: 247-254 violas double horns 1-2 on movement I's second-theme head (each theme gets a string colour, Adler II PDF 178); held brass, tuba, contrabassoon and Tpt 3 f under the two ff heads (PDF 184). 255-262 violas in repeated quarters under the chorale (PDF 181).
% c010: 254 first half Vla and Tpt 3 F sharp: the flat VI becomes the German sixth A flat-C-E flat-F sharp of bar 1, resolving to V7-I (A&S PDF 593; cyclic-notes: movement I bars 10-13 enlarged).
% c127 (texture; Adler ch. 17, PDF 274: keep accompanying forces registrally distant from the principal line; PDF 260: the figuration lies under the solo; PDF 279: Bizet doubles above the voice only at the climax): 257-262 Fl 2 joins Fl 1 on the chorale top line (breath after 260, dovetailed with Fl 1 after 258 and 261, so the top flute octave never stops; Adler2 PDF 188); its arpeggio, which peaked a fourth above the chorale (A6, A flat 6, G6), goes to Cl 1 at Vln II's pitch (mixed colour kept, c117), below the line. 257-258 violas C4 with Hn 3 and Tpt 3 instead of re-striking the chorale's E4 (c074).
