% c035 (engraving; Gould PDF 65-67): 262-264 Tpt 1 (B-C) lies below Tpt 2 (D-E); as semibreves on the shared staff the crossing was invisible and Tpt 1's protected B-C read as the lower
% note of a chord, so both parts are written as tied minims (stems show each part). 273: the semibreve C (Fl 1, Cl 1, Tpt 1) is moved right of the unison minim of the second player,
% so no semibreve touches a stem (Gould PDF 66). Sound unchanged.
% c046 (Adler PDF 292, 309, 321): breath marks after 250 and 254 in Tpt 1-2 and Hn 1-2 (two 4-bar ff slurs and the chorale entry back to back)
% c071 (melody; Adler PDF 20, Hindemith saves the tuba for a fresh statement): Tpt 1-2 rest at 269-270 (third F1 head, already in violins and winds) and play F1's continuation 271-272 instead; breath before Largamente. c071 (Adler PDF 24, last phrase not muddy): Bsn 1 E-Eb-F-E at 273-276 up an octave (E4, with Hn 4), out of the C3-Eb3 close third.
% c076 (Rimsky-Korsakov ch. I, PDF 37 and 44: give wind and brass players a little rest from time to time): Tpt 1-2 rest 253-254 (Ob 1-2 keep the S head at their pitch), so they come fresh to the chorale at 255 after 243-252 without a bar's rest
% c086 (playability; Sebesky ch. 2, PDF 42-43 and 51: a doubled lead line is not dropped mid-phrase, only at the dividing line between two phrases; PDF 44: rest the lead player):
% Tpt 1-2 rest the whole second S-head phrase 251-254 (they rested only 253-254 and left the phrase in its middle); Ob 1, Cl 1 and Hn 1-2 carry it at pitch. Four bars' rest before the chorale instead of two.
% c086 (Sebesky PDF 71: oboes are not voiced in unison, and the register above the high D is not written in leaps): Ob 2 rests 265-268 (both oboes leapt G5-E6 in unison ff); Ob 1 keeps the head with Fl, Cl, Vln.
% c086 (Sebesky PDF 23 fn. 1 and 46: one trumpet at mf+ has twice a horn's power; PDF 33: two horns in unison are strong): Hn 1 plays F1 at 265-268 an octave lower with Hn 2 and the cellos
% (it doubled Tpt 1-2 at the unison on written G5 ff after 22 bars of ff). PDF 46 (horns speak late, so a quick pickup is left out where other instruments carry it):
% Hn 3-4 enter on the E4 at 269 and 271 without the eighth-note G3 (Tbn 1-2, Bn 1, Vla, Vc keep it). PDF 46 (climactic cadence: two horns on each line, minor sixth inside the section):
% 277-279 Hn 1+3 C5 and Hn 2+4 E4 (were C5-G4-E4-C4 one per line); Hn 3 already held C5 at 273-275, so the high fff note goes to the two high horns.
finaleTptOne = { e''2.~(\f e''8 d''8 | c''4 a''2-- g''4 | f''4. e''8 d''4 f''8 a''8 | c'''2 b''2) | R1*4 | g'1\ff~ | g'1 | e''1~ | e''1 \breathe | \partCombineUnisono d''1\<~ | d''1 | c''1 | b'1 | c''1\fff~ | c''2.\> r4\! \partCombineAutomatic | c''2->(\ff g'4. a'8 | bes'4-.) a'8( g'8 f'4-.) e'4-. | e''2->( b'4. c''8 | d''4-.) c''8( b'8 a'4-.) g'4-. | \partCombineApart R1*2 | \partCombineAutomatic d'4(\ff g'8 a'8 b'4 d''4) | a''2-> g''4( f''4) \breathe | \once \override NoteColumn.force-hshift = #1.2 c''1->~ | c''1 | c''4->\< g'8-. g'8-. c''4-. e''4->~( | e''4 d''8 des''8 c''4-.) r4 | c''4-> r8 d''8 c''4-> r4 | c''1->\fff~ | c''4 r4 r2\fermata | }
% c064 (orchestration; Adler ch. 11, PDF 15: above mf one trumpet equals two horns; PDF 38-40, Ex. XI-21, Tchaikovsky 4/i 277-290: the long-note cantus firmus of a climax is doubled in unison in its brass):
% 255-258 Tpt 2 doubles Tpt 1 on the last chorale's first two notes (G4, E5; was E4, C5), so the protected top line is no longer one trumpet against Hn 1-2 a2, Tbn 1 and Tbn 2 on G3 below it;
% E stays in Hn 3, B.tbn, tuba, Vc, Vln II at 255-256 and C in Ob 2, Fl 2, Tbn 2, Vln II at 257-258; from 259 Tpt 2 resumes C5 (the only C at 259-260).
% c069 (Adler2 PDF 176, Jupiter coda: an established colour is kept intact while the line lasts): 259-260 Tpt 2 stays on the chorale line with Tpt 1 (D5) through 261; the seventh C of the ii-half-dim 6/5 goes to Ob 2 (C5) and Hn 4 (C4).
finaleTptTwo = { c''2.~(\f c''8 b'8 | a'4 f''2-- e''4 | d''4. c''8 a'4 d''8 f''8 | e''2 d''2) | R1*4 | g'1\ff~ | g'1 | e''1~ | e''1 \breathe | \partCombineUnisono d''1\<~ | d''1 | c''1 | b'1 | c''1\fff~ | c''2.\> r4\! \partCombineAutomatic | c''2->(\ff g'4. a'8 | bes'4-.) a'8( g'8 f'4-.) e'4-. | e''2->( b'4. c''8 | d''4-.) c''8( b'8 a'4-.) g'4-. | \partCombineApart R1*2 | \partCombineAutomatic d'4(\ff g'8 a'8 b'4 d''4) | a''2-> g''4( f''4) \breathe | \once \override NoteColumn.force-hshift = #0 c''2-> a'2 | aes'1-> | c''4->\< g'8-. g'8-. c''4-. e''4->~( | e''4 d''8 des''8 c''4-.) r4 | g'4-> r8 g'8 g'4-> r4 | g'1->\fff~ | g'4 r4 r2\fermata | }
% c051 (melody; Sebesky ch. 6, PDF 211, 213): 266, 268, 270 beat 3 Tpt 3 F4 with F1's F4 (Tpt 1-2, Hn 1-2, violas), not an accented E4 a semitone under it in the same instrument.
% c084 (Casanelles PDF 277: six brass on E4 masked the vi chord's third): 257-258 Tpt 3 C4 (was E4), with Hn 3
% c139 (Rimsky PDF 133: a brass basis stays an octave away from the brass melody): Tpt 3 247 C5, 248 F4, 249 C5 (were E4, A4, A4), so its repeated notes no longer sound the horns' S head (E4 at 247, tenuto A4 at 248) at the same pitch. 252 stays A4: F4 there makes fifths with the D5->C5 top line, C5 makes fifths with the basses.
